<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lindisfarne Gospels</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lindisfarne">Lindisfarne</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gospels">Gospels</a> is an illuminated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> of the four Gospels made at the monastery of Lindisfarne in Northumbria around 700–715, traditionally by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> Eadfrith. Its decorated carpet pages and initials combine Irish, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxons">Anglo-Saxon</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> styles. In the 10th century a priest named Aldred added an Old English gloss, the oldest surviving translation of the Gospels into English. It is held by the British Library.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lindisfarne-gospels.jpg" alt="Lindisfarne Gospels" width="320" height="454" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Eadfrith of Lindisfarne (presumed) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:LindisfarneFol27rIncipitMatt.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
